WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.960
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.780
Last week, the TED-Ed youtube channel, presented the frog-riddle,

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.080
which is a variation of a classic probability problem.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.860
However, in telling this story,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.680
they included a slight detail, which would actually change the calculation.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.700
In this video, I'm going to present, what I consider to be, the correct calculation.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.220
I'm going to go over a simplified version of the riddle, with pertinent details.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.880
I'm going to explain how it relates to a classic probability problem,

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.500
and one of its variations.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.920
And I'm going to show why the TED-Ed video, actually meant to do one calculation.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.140
But they presented the story for another calculation.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.440
So here's the riddle.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.700
Imagine you have a population of frogs that are equally likely to be male and female, and you cannot visually tell the difference between the genders.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.900
You see two frogs.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:00.920
what is the probability that there is a female in the pair of frogs, under two different circumstances:

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.000
Circumstance A: The pair of frogs contains a male frog.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.720
This is the calculation the TED-Ed video wanted to present.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.380
What the story actually presented, was variation B

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:23.020
The pair contains a male frog because you heard a croak that you know is distinctive to male frogs.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.380
It might seem to you, that variations A and B, are going to be the same.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.620
In either case you knows there's one male frog in the pair.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:38.820
But the detail, that you heard the croak of the frog, actually changes the probability.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:45.380
So, in order to explain why, I'm going to go over the classic probability problem this is based on.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.640
The Boy or Girl Paradox.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.520
Imagine Mr. Jones has two children.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.540
You want to know the probability that he has one girl, under two different variations.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.680
Variation A: He has a boy.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.740
Variation B: He has a boy born on Tuesday.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.280
It seems like these two variations would have the same probability.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.420
But let's go over the calculation to see why it's actually different between A and B.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.380
So variation A:

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.400
Lets consider Mr. Jones having two children.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.700
What's the sample space?

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.500
We know that he could have two boys.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.900
We know that he could have a boy and a girl.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.420
He could have a girl and a boy.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.900
Or he could have two girls.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.180
These are four events, and they're equally likely to happen,

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.980
if we assume the probability of having a boy and a girl is equal.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.500
If we know that he has a boy,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.220
the event, that he has two girls, is not possible.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.320
So we have a new sample space,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.460
in which there are three different events: he has two boys, a boy and a girl, or a girl and a boy.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.200
And these are equally likely events.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.960
What is the probability that he has a girl in this new sample space?

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.620
The events: boy/girls, and girl/boy are equally likely.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:15.000
There're two events in which he has a girl out of three.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:21.840
Therefore the probability, he has a girl, if we know he has a boy, is equal to two thirds.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.540
Now let's consider the other variation

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.060
What would happen if we know he has a boy that's born on a Tuesday.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.120
Now this seems like it shouldn't affect the calculation.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.860
Knowing he has a boy, obviously the boy would have to born on some day of the week.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:42.060
So whether he is born on Tuesday or any other day shouldn't matter.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:47.420
However the fact we learn, that the boy is born on a Tuesday, does change our sample space.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:54.900
So we know there're are only three possible pairs of genders, it could either be boy/boy, boy/girl, or girl/boy.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.440
He can't have two girls, because we know that he has a boy.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.020
We also know, that the boy's born on a Tuesday.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.100
So lets indicate the days of the week by a numerical code:

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.180
Zero equalling Sunday,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.700
one equalling Monday,

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.040
and counting all the way up until six equalling Saturday.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.160
We'll write the day of the week that a child is born, using a subscript.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.560
So what is our sample space now?

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.560
For boys that are—boys and girls that are born on different days of the week.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.020
We need to define the events that are equally likely to happen.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.120
If he has two boys, it's possible that the first child was born on a Tuesday,

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:44.380
and the second child was equally likely to be born on any of the other—any of the seven days of the week.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.540
It could also be that the older child is born on a Tuesday, and the f—

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:54.300
—the other child is born of any of days of the week, equally likely to happen.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.200
If he has a boy/girl, we know the boy has to be born on a Tuesday

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.740
and the girl can be born on any days of the week.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.000
And similarly for our last case.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.180
There's one thing to consider here actually:

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.920
We've written down B2B2... twice.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.280
We need to remove this, so that we're only considering equally likely events.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:22.820
We now have a list, of 27 equally likely events in our sample space.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:29.940
Out of these: seven plus seven involve Mr. Jones having a girl.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.440
Therefore we can calculate, the probability, has a girl, is 14 divided by 27,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.860
which is approximately 52%.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.260
And this is closer to our intuition

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:45.260
that, when he has two children, we suspect that it is equally likely,

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:50.520
knowing that one is a boy, that the other could be a girl, is almost 50%.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:55.180
So now lets relate that to the TED-Ed Frog Riddle.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.740
The video presented, the calculation for variation A.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:06.100
However, they actually presented the story of variation B

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.840
And this can be similar to the Boy or Girl Paradox, knowing that the boy was born on a Tuesday.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.260
So lets go through this calculation:

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.840
We know that if we have two frogs,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.400
They can either be: male/male, male/female, female/male, or female/female.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.840
If we know that there is a male frog in the pair,

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.720
we remove the female/female pair.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.200
And we end up with a new sample space of three equally likely events:

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.080
male/male, male/female, and female/male.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.560
In two of these events, there is a female.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:52.440
So we can calculate the probability there is a female is equal to two divided by three.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.620
Now this is the probability that is presented in the TED-Ed video.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:04.220
And it would be true, if you knew that in a pair of frogs there's a male frog, you learn that by random chance.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:12.620
However, the TED-Ed video presented the variation that you know that there's a male frog, because you heard a croak.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.520
So now we have specify a sample space that involves the male frog's croaking.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.140
We know that there are three different gender pairs that are possible:

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:26.260
male/male, male/female, or female/male.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:33.600
let's indicate a subscript of one, if we know that the frog croaks, and zero, if the frog does not croak.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:38.260
We have to consider the sample space of equally likely events.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:43.500
It could be that the first male frog croaked in a male/male pair.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.660
It could be that the second male frog croaked in the male/male pair

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:53.480
Or, in the male and female pairs, we would have that the male frog is croaking.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:08:00.140
The video doesn't tell us actually anything about the probability that male and female frogs are croaking.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:03.540
So we are going to assume that these are all equally likely events.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.520
The reason we have two different pairs of male/male frogs here,

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.060
is that either frog could be croaking.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.900
So in—If we consider this sample space of equally likely events,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.080
the probability that we have a female:

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:19.480
there're two different events out of four.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:23.680
So that means the probability is equal to two divided by four, which is one half.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:30.240
So summarizing: the TED-Ed Frog video,

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.700
actually presse—they wanted to do a calculation, for variation A.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:40.080
What's the probability that there's a female, if you know that the pair contains a male frog.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.120
This would be a probability of two thirds.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.780
What they actually presented,

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:51.180
was the variation, where you know there is a male frog, because you heard a croak that is distinctive to male frogs.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:54.460
And in that case the probability becomes one half.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:59.380
And intuitively, hearing the male croak changes the sample space.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:03.700
You are two times as likely to hear a male croak, from a male/male pair,

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:06.880
than from a female/male or a male/female pair.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:12.340
So I would suggest the TED-Ed Frog Video, as presented, should have a probability of one half.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.420
I understand they wanted to present variation A

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.760
Now the key in variation A is that you hear this information by random chance.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.240
Now that's actually pretty hard to do in practice, as you can see that's where they stumbled in the video and made a mistake.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.780
They included the detail: you have to learn somehow in practice.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:35.500
So ver—Very often in practice, you're most likely going to be presented with variation B.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.040
And that is going to be a probability of one half.

